window.AG_CHAPTERS["11"] = {"id":"11","title":"The Scribe Who Could Not Eat","titleZh":"寫不完的帳與吃不下的飯","patient":{"name":"Clerk Lin 林書辦","sprite":"man"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-town.jpg","key":"rain"},"pattern":"Liver invading the Spleen 肝脾不和","principle":"soothe the Liver and harmonise the Spleen","tongue":"pale body, thin white coating","pulseNote":{"en":"Straight and taut along its whole length, like a plucked string — and under the middle finger most of all.","zh":"端直以長，如按琴弦，尤顯於關部"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"LR3","zh":"太衝","py":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng","x":176,"y":962,"img":"art/body-leg-anterior.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":96,"y":700,"w":280,"h":459},"decoys":[{"x":220,"y":802},{"x":150,"y":1000},{"x":205,"y":930},{"x":176,"y":1006},{"x":246,"y":880}],"note":"<b>太衝 T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3</b> — on the dorsum of the foot, in the depression distal to the junction of the first and second metatarsal bones. Source point and Stream point of the Liver.<br>足背，第一、二蹠骨結合部之前凹陷中。肝之原穴、輸穴。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"Two complaints live in this man and they are not in the same place. Ask about the <b>ribs</b>, and ask about the <b>bowel</b> — then ask what makes each one worse.","zh":"其苦有二，不在一處。當問脅，當問腹，且問其加重之由。"},"look":{"en":"The tongue will be unremarkable, and that is itself the finding. A constrained Liver does not change the tongue much.","zh":"其舌平平，此亦一象。肝鬱者，舌多無顯變。"},"pulse":{"en":"One quality names the Liver more reliably than any other. Feel the <b>shape</b> of the vessel, not its speed.","zh":"辨肝之脈，一象最可憑。當察其形，不在其數。"},"dx":{"en":"Find which organ is at fault first, then name the <b>direction</b> the trouble travelled, then say how this differs from a plain weak Spleen.","zh":"先定何臟為本，次名其傳變之向，末辨其與單純脾虛何異。"},"rx":{"en":"Two organs, two jobs: something to loosen what is knotted, something to build what is weak.","zh":"二臟二事：一以解其結，一以建其虛。"},"tech":{"en":"One organ here is full and one is empty. Ask whether one technique can serve both.","zh":"本案一實一虛。當自問：一法可否兼之？"}},"reactions":[{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen watches him sigh twice before he has said anything.","zh":"參師父看他還沒開口就先嘆了兩口氣。"},{"who":"patient","en":"Clerk Lin presses his knuckles under his ribs, on the right, without seeming to notice.","zh":"林書辦用指節抵著右脅下，自己似乎沒察覺。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy fetches him water. He drinks half and sets it down.","zh":"藥童倒了水給他。他喝了半杯就放下。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"He keeps the grain ledgers. Three months behind, and the magistrate asks weekly.\"","zh":"「他管糧簿。積壓三月，縣裡每週來催。」"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"It comes on before I open the book,\" he says. \"Not after.\"","zh":"「還沒翻開帳簿就先發作，」他說，「不是翻開之後。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Note that sentence. The bowel is answering the mind, and the mind reaches it through the Liver.\"","zh":"「記下這句。腸應於心，而心之及腸，由肝而行。」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"excess_def","look":"excess_def","pulse":"excess_def","dx":"channel_organ","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"excess_def"},"reward":{"cardEn":"Wood Overacting on Earth","cardZh":"木剋土","cardTxt":"When the Liver fails to course freely it presses sideways into the Spleen. Treat the pressing organ and the pressed one in the same prescription — soothe what is knotted, build what is weak. Draining the Liver alone leaves the Spleen still weak; building the Spleen alone leaves it under the same pressure.","herbId":"fuling","herbEn":"Poria","herbZh":"茯苓","herbImg":"art/card-fuling.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Sweet, bland and neutral. It drains damp without draining strength and quiets the spirit — which is why it sits in Xiao Yao San, the classical formula for exactly this pattern, doing the Spleen's half of the work while other herbs soothe the Liver."},"endings":{"three":["A month later Clerk Lin brings the ledger to the clinic to show that it is finished. \"I still dread it,\" he says. \"But my stomach stopped arguing.\"<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You treated both organs. Most students drain the Liver, feel clever, and leave a Spleen that is still too weak to hold its ground.\"","一個月後，林書辦把帳簿帶到醫館，說是做完了。「我還是怕它，」他說，「但肚子不跟我吵了。」<br><b>參師父：</b>「你二臟同治。多數學生瀉了肝，自覺高明，卻留下一個仍然守不住的脾。」"],"two":["The rib-side fullness eases. The stool is still loose on bad days.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You loosened the knot. You did not finish strengthening what the knot had been squeezing.\"","脅下鬆了，遇事仍溏。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你解了其結。被結所迫者，你還沒有把它補起來。」"],"one":["No change. He is back within the fortnight.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"Which organ is full, and which is empty? Until you can say that, you cannot choose a technique.\"","未見好轉，不到半月又來了。<br><b>參師父：</b>「何臟為實？何臟為虛？此問未決，手法無從擇。」"],"titles":["Clerk Lin Finishes the Ledger","Clerk Lin Breathes Easier","Clerk Lin Will Need Another Visit"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"Every pattern you have treated so far sat in one organ. From today they come in pairs.","cn":"你至今所治，皆在一臟。自今日起，證來成對。"},{"w":"patient","en":"Doctor. Lin, clerk at the grain office. My ribs feel full and my belly is never settled.","cn":"大夫。姓林，糧房書辦。脅下脹滿，腹中總不安穩。"},{"w":"patient","en":"I get a griping pain, then I must go, and afterwards the pain eases. Then it comes back. And I have no appetite at all.","cn":"腹中絞痛，急欲如廁，瀉後則減，過些時又作。全無胃口。"},{"w":"master","en":"Rib-side fullness is one organ speaking. A loose bowel is another. Your task is to say which one started it.","cn":"脅脹者一臟之言，便溏者又一臟之言。你當說出：孰為先？"},{"w":"master","en":"And a warning: this looks very like the rice-shop ledger you treated in chapter four. It is not the same illness.","cn":"且戒之：此與第四章米行帳簿之案極相似，然非一病。"}],"qa":[{"q":"Where exactly is the fullness, and does it move?","cq":"脹滿在何處？其痛移否？","good":1,"a":"Under both sets of ribs, and across the belly. It wanders — never quite the same spot twice.","ca":"兩脅之下，並及腹中。痛無定處，每次不在同一點。","clue":{"en":"Wandering distending pain in the ribs and belly","zh":"胸脅腹脹痛、痛無定處"},"note":"Distending and <b>wandering</b> is stuck qi, not stasis and not deficiency. Blood stasis stabs and stays put; deficiency aches dully and welcomes pressure. Qi moves, so pain from stuck qi moves too. And the rib-side is the Liver channel's own territory. 脹而走竄者，氣滯也，非瘀非虛。瘀則刺痛有定處，虛則隱痛喜按。氣行故痛亦行。且脅肋為肝經所過。"},{"q":"What brings it on?","cq":"何事引發？","good":1,"a":"Pressure. The magistrate's clerk comes on Mondays and I am ill by Sunday night.","ca":"心裡有事就發。縣裡每逢週一來人，我週日夜裡就開始不適。","clue":{"en":"Brought on by emotional pressure","zh":"因情志而發"},"note":"<b>This is the finding that names the cause.</b> The Liver governs the free coursing of qi and is the organ most directly moved by sustained emotional strain. A digestive complaint that reliably tracks the mood is a Liver complaint wearing the Spleen's clothes. 此症定其病因。肝主疏泄，情志所傷，肝先受之。凡消化之疾隨情志而作者，其本在肝而見於脾。"},{"q":"What are your stools like, and does anything relieve the pain?","cq":"大便如何？何以得減？","good":1,"a":"Loose and urgent, and the pain eases right after. Some weeks I cannot go at all, then it swings back.","ca":"稀而急，瀉後痛即減。有時數日不行，過後又轉為瀉。","clue":{"en":"Loose urgent stool, pain relieved after; alternating with constipation","zh":"便溏而急、瀉後痛減、便秘泄瀉交替"},"note":"Two findings, both diagnostic. Pain relieved by passing stool says the obstruction is qi, not substance. And <b>alternating</b> constipation and diarrhoea is the classic signature of this pattern rather than of a simply weak Spleen, which is loose and stays loose. 二症皆可為憑。瀉後痛減，知其所滯為氣而非有形之物。便秘與泄瀉交替，正本證之特徵；單純脾虛則但溏而不變。"},{"q":"Do you belch or bring up sour fluid?","cq":"噯氣嗎？吞酸嗎？","good":0,"a":"I sigh a great deal. But no sourness, and my stomach itself feels all right.","ca":"常嘆息。但不吞酸，胃本身沒有不適。","clue":"","note":"An excellent exclusion, and it decides between two sibling patterns. Liver invading the <b>Stomach</b> gives belching, acid regurgitation and nausea — reflux. Liver invading the <b>Spleen</b> gives bloating and loose stool. Same aggressor, different victim, different formula. 排除得當，且分二鄰證。肝犯胃者噯氣、吞酸、噁心，即今之胃食道逆流；肝犯脾者腹脹便溏。所犯者同，所受者異，其方亦異。"},{"q":"Are you cold, and is your low back sore?","cq":"畏寒嗎？腰痠嗎？","good":0,"a":"No. Warm enough, and my back is fine.","ca":"不冷，腰也沒事。","clue":"","note":"A clean exclusion of the Kidney. Without cold, nocturia or a sore low back, this is not the yang-deficient picture you met in chapter ten. 排除腎之當否。無畏寒、無夜尿、無腰痠，則非第十章之陽虛證。"}],"look":[{"t":"Pale body, thin white coating, unremarkable","c":"舌淡、苔薄白，無甚異象","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"An unremarkable tongue","zh":"舌象平平"},"note":"Correct, and the lack of a finding <i>is</i> the finding. Qi stagnation is a functional disorder — nothing substantial has accumulated yet — so the tongue often stays near normal. When the tongue is quiet and the pulse is loud, believe the pulse. 是。無象即是一象。氣滯為功能之病，未成有形之積，故舌多如常。舌靜而脈顯者，當從脈。"},{"t":"Red body with a yellow greasy coat","c":"舌紅、苔黃膩","ok":0,"note":"That is damp-heat with substance behind it. He has no fever, no thirst, no burning stool. 此為濕熱有形之象。本案不發熱、不口渴、便亦不灼。"},{"t":"Pale swollen body with tooth marks","c":"舌淡胖有齒痕","ok":0,"note":"That is the Spleen deficiency of chapter four, and it is the trap here — the Spleen is involved, but as the victim, not the origin, and it has not yet swollen. 此第四章脾虛之象，亦本案之陷阱：脾雖與焉，然為所犯者而非病之始，其舌尚未胖。"},{"t":"Purple body with stasis spots","c":"舌紫暗、有瘀斑","ok":0,"note":"Long-standing qi stagnation can become stasis, and then you would see this. His has not yet. 氣滯日久可成血瘀，其時方見此象。本案未至。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Wiry 弦","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Wiry pulse","zh":"脈弦"},"note":"Correct — and it is the single most useful pulse in the book, because it names an organ rather than a quality. Wiry is the Liver's own pulse: straight, taut and long, like pressing a lute string. Felt most at the middle position, which is where the Liver answers on the left wrist. 是。此為書中最可用之脈，以其能指一臟而非徒言一性。弦為肝之本脈：端直以長，如按琴弦。左關候肝，故關部尤顯。"},{"t":"Weak 弱","ok":0,"note":"A weak pulse is pure deficiency. His Spleen is weak, but the Liver is <b>full</b> — and a full organ does not give a weak pulse. Reading only the weakness would lead you to tonify a patient who also needs draining. 弱者純虛之脈。本案脾雖虛而肝為實，實不見弱。但取其弱，則於當瀉者而純補之。"},{"t":"Slippery 滑","ok":0,"note":"Slippery means something substantial rolls in the vessel — phlegm, food, damp. Stuck qi has no substance to it. 滑者脈中有形之物往來，痰、食、濕也。氣滯無形。"},{"t":"Thready and rapid 細數","ok":0,"note":"Yin deficiency with empty heat. Nothing here is hot and nothing is short of substance. 陰虛內熱之脈。本案無熱，亦無虧。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — which organ is at fault first?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：何臟為本？</small>","opts":[{"t":"The Liver — its qi is constrained","c":"肝 — 肝氣鬱結","ok":1,"note":"Correct. Three findings put the Liver first: the complaint tracks his mood, the pain wanders and distends, and the pulse is wiry. Note that all three are about <i>movement</i> — which is exactly the function the Liver governs. 是。三症俱指於肝：隨情志而作、痛脹而走竄、脈弦。三者皆言「行」，而行者正肝之所主。"},{"t":"The Spleen — its qi is weak","c":"脾 — 脾氣虛","ok":0,"note":"The Spleen is certainly involved — the appetite and the stool say so. But a Spleen that failed on its own does not produce rib-side pain, a wiry pulse, or symptoms that arrive on Sunday night. It is the second organ here, not the first. 脾誠與焉：納呆、便溏可證。然脾自病者不致脅痛、不致脈弦、亦不擇時而發。於此為第二臟，非其始。"},{"t":"The Stomach — its qi is rebelling","c":"胃 — 胃氣上逆","ok":0,"note":"Then he would belch and regurgitate acid. He does neither, and he said his stomach itself feels fine. 若然，當噯氣吞酸。本案二者皆無，且其言胃本無恙。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — name the direction the trouble travelled.<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：其傳變之向為何？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Wood overacting on Earth 相乘 — the Liver presses into the Spleen","c":"木乘土（相乘）— 肝橫逆犯脾","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct, and the direction word is what the question is really testing.</b> Wood already controls Earth; when Wood is too strong that normal restraint turns into damage. That is <b>overacting 相乘</b> — excessive control, running <i>along</i> the controlling cycle. Boards love to swap this word with its opposite. 是。方向之詞正本題所考。木本剋土，木過強則常剋轉為乘。相乘者，剋之太過，順剋道而行。考題最愛以其反義相易。"},{"t":"Earth insulting Wood 相侮 — the Spleen pushes back on the Liver","c":"土侮木（相侮）— 脾反侮肝","ok":0,"note":"Insulting runs <b>backwards</b> along the controlling cycle: the controlled element pushing back at its controller. Here the Liver is the aggressor, so the direction is the other way. Getting this word backwards is the commonest way to lose this question. 相侮者逆剋道而行：所勝者反侮其所不勝。本案肝為侵者，其向適反。此字顛倒，即失此題。"},{"t":"Mother-child transmission 母病及子 — along the generating cycle","c":"母病及子 — 循相生而傳","ok":0,"note":"Liver and Spleen are not mother and child. Wood generates Fire, not Earth. Their relationship is controller and controlled. 肝脾非母子。木生火，不生土。二者為剋與被剋。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — how does this differ from the plain Spleen qi deficiency of chapter four?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：此與第四章單純脾氣虛何異？</small>","opts":[{"t":"There is a full organ here as well as an empty one","c":"此案一實一虛，非純虛","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and it changes the treatment completely. Zhou Bo's Spleen was simply empty — everything was tonified. Clerk Lin has an empty Spleen <i>and</i> a full Liver, so the prescription must drain in one place and build in another. Add the emotional trigger, the wandering rib pain, the wiry pulse and the alternating bowel, and the two cases stop looking alike. 是，且治法全異。周伯之脾但虛耳，通案皆補。林書辦則脾虛而肝實，故一方之中，有瀉有補。更兼情志之誘、脅痛之走、脈弦、便之或秘或瀉，二案遂判然可分。"},{"t":"There is no real difference — both are Spleen patterns","c":"實無異，二者皆脾證","ok":0,"note":"Then you would tonify throughout and leave the Liver pressing on the Spleen you have just built up. He would improve briefly and relapse — which is exactly what happens in clinic when this is missed. 若然，則通案純補，而肝之壓迫如故。其人暫愈而復發——臨床誤此者，正是如此。"},{"t":"This one is cold and the earlier one was not","c":"此案有寒，前案無寒","ok":0,"note":"Neither case is cold. He is not chilled, his stool is not watery, and his tongue is not pale and swollen. 二案皆無寒。其人不畏寒、便非清稀、舌亦不淡胖。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"LR3","t":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3 太衝","ok":1,"note":"Source point of the Liver and the principal point for soothing constrained Liver qi. It treats the aggressor directly. Paired with LI4 it forms the Four Gates 四關, the classical combination for moving qi through the whole body. 肝之原穴，疏肝解鬱之主穴，直治其侵者。與合谷相配為「四關」，古之行一身氣機之要方。"},{"id":"ST36","t":"Z&#250;s&#257;nl&#464; ST36 足三里","ok":1,"note":"The great tonifying point of the middle burner. It treats the organ being pressed — build the Spleen so it can hold its ground, rather than only removing the pressure. 補中焦之要穴，治其所受者。健其脾使能自守，不徒去其所迫。"},{"id":"GB34","t":"Y&#225;ngl&#237;ngqu&#225;n GB34 陽陵泉","ok":1,"note":"Sea point of the Gallbladder and Influential point of the sinews. Liver and Gallbladder are an interior-exterior pair, so this reaches the same constraint from the yang side — and it is a standard partner for rib-side pain. 膽之合穴，又為筋會。肝膽相表裡，故可自陽經解其鬱。脅痛之常配。"},{"id":"SP9","t":"Y&#299;nl&#237;ngqu&#225;n SP9 陰陵泉","ok":0,"note":"Drains damp from the lower burner, and it drains. There is no damp here — no heaviness, no greasy coat — and draining an already weak Spleen is the wrong direction. 利下焦之濕，其法主瀉。本案無濕：不身重、苔不膩。脾本虛而瀉之，為逆。"},{"id":"LU5","t":"Ch&#464;z&#233; LU5 尺澤","ok":0,"note":"Sea point of the Lung. Nothing in this case is in the chest or the breath. 肺之合穴。本案不涉胸中，不涉呼吸。"},{"id":"KI3","t":"T&#224;ix&#299; KI3 太谿","ok":0,"note":"Source point of the Kidney. Reasonable if there were cold, nocturia or a sore low back — he has none of them, and he told you so. 腎之原穴。若見畏寒、夜尿、腰痠則可取；本案三者皆無，其人已明言。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Drain the Liver points and reinforce the Spleen points","c":"肝穴用瀉，脾穴用補","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct, and this is the first case in the grove where one technique will not do.</b> Every chapter so far has been wholly full or wholly empty. Here the Liver is full and the Spleen is empty in the same patient, so the needle technique changes point by point: reducing at LR3 and GB34, reinforcing at ST36. Read the organ, not the patient, when choosing a technique. 是。此為杏林諸案中，首見一法不足以應者。前此諸章，非全實即全虛。今肝實而脾虛並見於一人，故手法隨穴而異：太衝、陽陵泉用瀉，足三里用補。擇法者當視其臟，不徒視其人。"},{"t":"Reinforce everything, since he is thin and tired","c":"通案用補，以其形瘦而倦","ok":0,"note":"Then you tonify the Liver that is already too strong, and the pressure on the Spleen increases. This is the commonest error in this pattern. 若然，則補其本已過強之肝，脾所受之迫益甚。此證之最常誤者。"},{"t":"Drain everything, since the pain is worse with pressure","c":"通案用瀉，以其痛拒按","ok":0,"note":"Then you drain a Spleen that is already too weak to hold its ground. The rib pain resists pressure; the belly does not — read them separately. 若然，則瀉其本已不足以自守之脾。脅痛拒按而腹不拒按，當分而讀之。"},{"t":"Even method throughout","c":"通案平補平瀉","ok":0,"note":"An even technique when you know which organ is full and which is empty is a decision not to decide. The whole point of naming both is to treat them differently. 虛實既明而用平補平瀉，是不決之決。並舉二臟者，正為異治之。"}]};
